<h2>The argument for simply running slower</h2>

<p>Faced with all this, there is a valid and frequent objection: the easiest way to avoid the wall is not eating 90 grams of carbohydrates per hour or training chronic load. The easiest way is simply to run slower from the starting gun.</p>

<p>And it is true. If you adjust your race pace 30 or 40 seconds per kilometer (45 to 60 seconds per mile) slower than your actual aerobic threshold, the demand on fast-twitch fibers disappears. You rely almost exclusively on fat oxidation. At that intensity, your glycogen reserves will last 5 or 6 hours. There will be no wall, because the engine will never work at the RPMs that cause it.</p>

<p>But you do not wake up early five days a week for four months, you do not structure your weekends around 30-kilometer (18-mile) long runs, and you do not weigh your food just to survive the distance with a conservative jog. You make that effort to compete and discover the real limit of your physical capacity.</p>

<p>Drastically lowering your pace is a survival tactic. Building durability through load management and training your gut to absorb energy at high intensity is the strategy that allows you to race the marathon, not just finish it.</p>
